menu "GBA SPI-slave front-end (experimental)"

config GBA_SPI_DIRECT_SELFTEST
    bool "Run the GBA SPI-slave self-test at boot (skips LDN)"
    default n
    help
        When set, app_main() runs gba_spi_selftest() (design doc §8 gate 1/2) instead of the LDN
        join, logging the 32-bit words the GBA clocks in so the SPI mode + wiring can be confirmed.
        Leave OFF for the normal two-chip LDN firmware.

config GBA_SPI_TRADE_RELAY_SELFTEST
    bool "Run the Phase 1 trade-relay self-test at boot (skips LDN)"
    default n
    help
        When set, app_main() runs gba_spi_trade_relay_selftest() instead of the LDN join: the bare
        core1 GBA adapter wired to the REAL gba_relay backend with a synthetic Switch peer seeded,
        so the discover->connect->slot-exchange flow can be validated against a real cartridge in
        the TRADE counter, without real Wi-Fi yet (docs/16-single-chip-trade-plan.md, Phase 1).
        Mutually exclusive with GBA_SPI_DIRECT_SELFTEST in practice (both start core1). Leave OFF
        for the normal LDN firmware.

config GBA_SPI_BUS_PROBE
    bool "Run the GPIO bus-stall probe at boot (skips LDN)"
    default n
    help
        When set, app_main() runs gba_spi_bus_probe() instead of the LDN join: bare core 1 times
        every back-to-back GPIO_IN_REG read (ccount deltas, min/max/threshold buckets) while
        core 0 cycles 5-second load phases (quiet, UART log spam, flash-read churn, PSRAM memcpy
        churn, all-at-once). Answers docs/17 section 7's open question on-device: can core-0 bus
        traffic stall a core-1 GPIO read past the 250ns half-bit budget? No GBA needs to be
        connected. Mutually exclusive with the other GBA_SPI_* selftests. Leave OFF normally.

config GBA_SPI_MODE
    int "SPI slave mode (0-3) for the GBA link"
    default 3
    range 0 3
    help
        SPI CPOL/CPHA for the GBA link. Start at 3 (design doc §2); if login words read bit-shifted
        or garbled, try 0. This is the first go/no-go dial.

endmenu
